#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124184 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8702032e-0795-5b51-9cc9-f89b35b64fa0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where a Partial Derivative Exists"}] :question/id #uuid "a663f68e-1fb9-5653-a128-1b55c2a58769" :question/math-academy-id "e-3449" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac yx$, find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ exists." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the partial derivative of a function exists where the original function is defined *and* the partial derivative is defined.\n\nFirst, notice that $f(x,y)$ is not defined when $x=0$. So, the domain of $f$ is\n\n$$\nD_f = \\{(x,y) \\,: \\, x\\ne 0 \\}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{y}(x, y) &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ is also not defined for $x=0$. So, the domain of $f_y$ is\n\n$$\nD_{f_y} = \\{(x,y) \\,: \\, x\\ne 0 \\}\n$$\n\nTo compute the set where\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\n$$\n\nexists, we take the intersection of the domain of the original function $(D_f)$ with the domain of the partial derivative $(D_{f_y})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{f}∩D_{f_{y}} &= {(x, y): x \\ne 0}∩{(x, y): x \\ne 0} \\\\\n&= {(x, y): x \\ne 0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\n$$\n\nexists on the set\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,: \\, x \\neq 0 \\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124185 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/id #uuid "7297f213-2db2-5da2-9bba-cac7e31f6b64" :question/math-academy-id "e-3450" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x,y,z)=xz\\ln{(x+y+z)}$?\n\n1. $f_x(-1,-1,-1)$ is defined\n2. $f_y(1,0,-1)$ is defined\n3. $f_z(1,1,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= z\\ln (x + y + z) + \\frac{xz}{x + y + z} \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y} &= \\frac{xz}{x + y + z} \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z} &= x\\ln (x + y + z) + \\frac{xz}{x + y + z}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(-1,-1,-1)$ into $f$, we get\n$f(-1,-1,-1) = (-1)(-1)\\ln{(-1-1-1)} = \\ln{(-3)}$,\nwhich is undefined since the logarithm exists only for positive arguments. Therefore, $f_x$ cannot exist at this point.\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0,-1)$ into $f_y$, we get\n$f(1,0,-1) = \\dfrac{(1)(-1)}{1+0-1} = \\dfrac{-1}{0}$,\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1,1)$:\n$f(1,1,1)= (1)(1)\\ln(1+1+1)=\\ln(3)$\nLikewise $f_z$ is also defined at $(1,1,1)$:\n$f_{z}(1, 1, 1)|= (1)\\ln (1 + 1 + 1) + ((1)(1))/(1 + 1 + 1) = \\ln (3) + \\frac{1}{3}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124284 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where All Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/id #uuid "97192aac-6c1a-5ead-962b-0bf7ab573661" :question/math-academy-id "e-6109" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)= \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y^2}\\right)$ and\n$\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = -\\dfrac{2}{y}$,\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the partial derivatives of a function exist where the original function is defined and the partial derivatives are defined.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y^2}\\right)\n$$\n\nis defined only when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{y^2}>0\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is\n\n$$\nD_f = \\{(x,y) \\,: \\, x > 0, \\: y \\neq 0 \\}\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following:\n\n- The partial derivative $f_x(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x \\neq 0$. So, the domain of $f_x$ is\n$D_{f_x} = \\{(x,y) \\,: \\, x \\neq 0 \\}$.\n- The partial derivative $f_y(x,y) = -\\dfrac{2}{y}$ is defined only when $y \\neq 0$. So, the domain of $f_y$ is\n$D_{f_y} = \\{(x,y) \\,: \\, y \\neq 0 \\}$.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{f}∩D_{f_{x}}∩D_{f_{y}} &= {(x, y): x > 0, y \\ne 0}∩{(x, y): x \\ne 0}∩{(x, y): y \\ne 0} \\\\\n&= {(x, y): x > 0, y \\ne 0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e24fbb73-65ac-5d20-9022-e9c395b73c01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122489} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c36eb3-aaee-574a-965e-d41edfa0163a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122490} {:answer/id #uuid "012bb3b9-97ef-506b-8831-7c6dc9daa973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122491} {:answer/id #uuid "09e968ff-4f3a-599a-a516-5cfbb48afc82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122492} {:answer/id #uuid "264b09c2-891a-5274-ad8f-7cced35bdf2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "012bb3b9-97ef-506b-8831-7c6dc9daa973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122491} :answer-field/id #uuid "e60a9e2b-e34b-5b0f-bb3c-0faa4d1f8e21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122488}] :question/id #uuid "cee8f940-b17d-518d-bf59-666639ac6929" :question/math-academy-id "q-36504" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x, y) = \\ln (\\frac{x}{x + y})$?\n\n1. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1, 1)$\n2. $f_{x}(0, 1)$ is defined\n3. $f_{y}(0, 1)$ is defined"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84c64be2-8a56-5b6c-ae96-f9d4645ac4e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122519} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d03fa6-77ec-5829-8b10-ec692e18d2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122520} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1c6212-87fd-515a-a1f2-4bef6f61a05d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122521} {:answer/id #uuid "2f437b98-c608-547f-8ae5-06d180b1edd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122522} {:answer/id #uuid "423eff7f-f33f-5a1e-80ce-c5b2da5d7bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "423eff7f-f33f-5a1e-80ce-c5b2da5d7bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122523} :answer-field/id #uuid "66794817-bc48-561a-b08a-87b4a597cf52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122518}] :question/id #uuid "ff5209de-df39-5705-8c96-c53acb60628b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36522" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x, y, z) = \\frac{z^{2}}{x^{2} - y^{2}}$?\n\n1. The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(1,-1, 0)$\n2. $f_{x}(0, 1, 1)$ is defined\n3. $f_{z}(1, 1, 0)$ is defined"}]}
